## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design pluggable runtime extensions, handle concurrency considerations, and understand trade-offs between in-process versus out-of-process sinks.

## Key Concepts

- Plugin registry and dynamic loading at runtime
- Minimal interface contracts for telemetry sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and failure isolation
- Stateless versus stateful sinks and lifecycle management

## Code Example

```javascript
interface TelemetrySink {
  send(event: TelemetryEvent): Promise<void>;
  flush?(): Promise<void>;
  shutdown?(): Promise<void>;
}

class PluginRegistry {
  #sinks = new Map();

  register(name, factory) {
    this.#sinks.set(name, factory(this));
  }

  get(name) {
    return this.#sinks.get(name);
  }

  list() {
    return Array.from(this.#sinks.keys());
  }
}

class SyslogSink {
  #buffer = [];
  #batchSize = 100;
  #flushInterval = 5000;
  #paused = false;
  #controller = new AbortController();

  constructor(options = {}) {
    this.#batchSize = options.batchSize ?? 100;
    this.#flushInterval = options.flushInterval ?? 5000;
    this.#startFlushTimer();
  }

  async send(event) {
    if (this.#paused) {
      throw new Error('Backpressure: sink is paused');
    }

    this.#buffer.push(event);

    if (this.#buffer.length >= this.#batchSize) {
      await this.flush();
    }

    if (this.#buffer.length > this.#batchSize * 0.8) {
      this.#paused = true;
    }
  }

  async flush() {
    if (this.#buffer.length === 0) return;

    const batch = this.#buffer.splice(0, this.#batchSize);
    try {
      await this.#sendToSyslog(batch);
      this.#paused = false;
    } catch (err) {
      this.#buffer.unshift(...batch);
      throw err;
    }
  }

  async shutdown() {
    this.#controller.abort();
    await this.flush();
  }

  #startFlushTimer() {
    setInterval(() => this.flush(), this.#flushInterval);
  }

  async #sendToSyslog(batch) {
    // Send batch to syslog endpoint
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PluginRegistry();
registry.register('syslog', () => new SyslogSink({ batchSize: 50 }));
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design pluggable, runtime-extendable components with clean isolation between tenants and core logic. It exercises registry design, immutable pipelines, and safe runtime extension.

## Key Concepts
- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry + factory pattern
- Runtime extensibility with per-tenant manifests
- Immutability and thread-safety in pipelines

## Code Example
```javascript
class ILogProcessor {
  async process(entry, next) { throw new Error('not implemented') }
}

class ProcessorRegistry {
  constructor(){ this.factories = new Map() }
  register(name, factory){ this.factories.set(name, factory) }
  buildPipeline(names){
    let next = async () => {}
    for (let i = names.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const F = this.factories.get(names[i])
      const proc = new F()
      const prev = next
      next = async () => { await proc.process(null, prev) }
    }
    return next
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle processor ordering changes at runtime while preserving ongoing log streams?
- How would you test pipelines for tenant isolation and regressions when new processors are added?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of the open/closed principle, polymorphism, and runtime extensibility. It also checks the ability to add new parsing backends without touching the core importer.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern
- Registry/Plugin registry
- Open-Closed principle
- Dependency injection

## Code Example
```javascript
class Parser {
  parse(input) {
    throw new Error('not implemented');
  }
}

class CSVParser extends Parser {
  parse(input) {
    // CSV parsing logic
    return [];
  }
}

class YAMLParser extends Parser {
  parse(input) {
    // YAML parsing logic
    return [];
  }
}

class ParserRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.parsers = new Map();
  }

  register(extension, parser) {
    this.parsers.set(extension, parser);
  }

  get(extension) {
    return this.parsers.get(extension);
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new ParserRegistry();
registry.register('.csv', new CSVParser());
registry.register('.yaml', new YAMLParser());

// Core importer (unchanged)
function importFile(filePath) {
  const ext = path.extname(filePath);
  const parser = registry.get(ext);
  return parser.parse(fs.readFileSync(filePath));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design pluggable, runtime-extensible routing without changing core.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern
- Registry for dynamic extensibility
- Runtime plugin loading
- Trade-offs: cold-start, security, versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal TypeScript-ish example
interface RoutingStrategy { route(req: any, handlers: any): string }
class StrategyRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => RoutingStrategy>()
  register(name: string, factory: () => RoutingStrategy) { this.map.set(name, factory) }
  get(name: string, fallback: () => RoutingStrategy) { return (this.map.get(name) || fallback)() }
}
class Router { constructor(private registry: StrategyRegistry, private defaultName: string) {} route(req: any, handlers: any) { const strat = this.registry.get(this.defaultName, () => null as any); return strat?.route(req, handlers) }
}
```

